e by experiment groups and include all the planned experiments.\n\nFor the cutoff experiments, I have some actual evaluation results: s0 shows TSR 0.912 and CAR 0.768, s1 shows TSR 0.918 and CAR 0.798, and s2 is still training so I'll need to predict those values. For the multi-cutoff dose-response experiments, the median tier predicts no clear trend across cutoff points, so I should expect the CAR values to cluster around 0.755-0.790 without any monotonic pattern.\n\nBut looking at the actual numbers—0.758 at 15K, 0.770 at 25K, 0.777 at 35K, and 0.782 at 45K—there actually is a monotonic trend, which contradicts my initial median tier interpretation.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the cutoff@15K case more carefully. At 15K steps (30% of training), the occupancy loss weight has decayed to about 0.00741, so the gradient signal has been flowing through the occupancy head for a while before it gets removed entirely. The median tier prediction would be that these differences should exist but be statistically indistinguishable with typical error bars overlapping substantially—adding even moderate variance around 0.025 would wash out the significance of these small gaps.\n\nFor cutoff@45K, we're at 90% of training with the loss weight already quite small at 0.00407, so the result should track very close to the full OCC baseline. Sketching out the dose-response curve across these cutoff points shows a clear monotonic trend: earlier cutoffs like 15K give modest protection around 0.758, while later cutoffs approach the full OCC performance of 0.797, with the detach baseline sitting lower at 0.746 as the floor.\n\nBut here's the catch — these are single-seed experiments with variance around ±0.025 per point, which means the 95% confidence intervals would overlap substantially across all conditions. There's no statistical power to distinguish whether the effect is truly gradual or just noise. That's the core limitation of this tier.\n\nNow I need to think through the gradient and CK